<!-- Airhub vs Saily Comparison Content -->
<div class="content-section">

  {% set airhubPrimary = providerA.codes | findCode(providerA.primaryCode) %}
  {% set airhubBest = providerA.codes | bestValidCode %}
  {% set sailyPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Airhub</strong> if you want the cheapest possible price per gigabyte and like that SMS and a local number come bundled in rather than sold as an extra. Pick <strong>Saily</strong> if you'd normally pay for a VPN and ad-blocker separately when traveling anyway, or you're crossing several borders on one trip and don't want to juggle a new eSIM at each one. Both sit in our "Cheap" price bracket, but they get there in different ways.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: Per-GB Blocks With Extras, or One Border-Crossing eSIM With a VPN Built In</h2>
  <p>
    Airhub sells data in multi-gigabyte blocks (3GB, 4GB) rather than the single-day or single-GB units most competitors use, and in cheaper destinations that lands at genuinely low per-GB prices. It also throws in SMS and, in many destinations, a local phone number on top of the data, which most budget eSIM apps don't. What it doesn't do is bundle any security features, and you're still picking a fresh package per destination.
  </p>
  <p>
    Saily is NordVPN's eSIM spin-off, built by the same company (Nord Security), so every plan ships with ad blocking, malware/phishing site blocking, and a virtual-location tool baked in. It also sells one universal eSIM instead of a separate profile per region: install it once and it keeps working as you cross into a new supported country. The tradeoff is price per gigabyte, which runs noticeably higher than Airhub's in most destinations we checked.
  </p>

