      <li><strong>Saily:</strong> {{ providerB.primaryCode }}, {{ sailyPrimary.discount | lower }}. <a href="/saily/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:.75rem;margin-bottom:0;color:#63553F;">Neither code is a deep discount on its own; both providers' real savings come from their base pricing, not the percentage off.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Airhub or Saily the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Airhub if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You want the lowest per-GB price, especially in Thailand, Japan, or similar cheap destinations</li>
        <li>You want SMS and a local number bundled with your data instead of sold as an add-on</li>
        <li>You roughly know how much data you'll use per destination and don't mind picking a new package at each one</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Saily if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You'd normally pay for a VPN or ad-blocker separately when traveling anyway</li>
        <li>Multi-country trip where you'd rather not reinstall an eSIM at every border</li>
        <li>You're a frequent flyer who could actually use the Ultra tier's lounge passes</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Saily's "unlimited" has a daily ceiling.</strong> Its fair-use policy caps full speed at 5GB in a rolling 24-hour window in most destinations (3GB in some), then throttles to 1Mbps until the local-midnight reset. Airhub doesn't sell unlimited plans at all, so there's no equivalent cap to worry about there, but you also don't get the no-cap peace of mind: you're buying a fixed block and need to watch it run out.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Neither is the pick for a full phone-number replacement.</strong> Airhub's local number covers SMS but not voice calls. Saily doesn't include a local number or SMS at all. If you specifically need a number with calls guaranteed, <a href="/simlocal/">SimLocal</a>'s UK plans include one and we've bought and confirmed it. If you'd rather skip the GB-counting entirely on either of these, <a href="/holafly/">Holafly</a>'s flat-rate unlimited plans are the alternative worth a look.
  </p>
  <p>
    For device setup and eSIM basics that apply to both providers, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2>Airhub vs Saily FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is cheaper, Airhub or Saily</h3>
      <p>Airhub, in most destinations we checked. It sells data in multi-GB blocks that work out to a lower per-GB price than Saily's single-GB pricing, especially in cheaper destinations like Thailand and Japan. Saily's Global plan is the one exception where its per-GB price comes out lower.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does Airhub include a VPN or security features</h3>
      <p>No. Airhub is data (plus SMS and, in many destinations, a local number) with no bundled security tools. Saily includes ad blocking, malware/phishing site blocking, and a virtual-location tool on every plan, since it's built by NordVPN's parent company.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Is Saily's unlimited data really unlimited</h3>
      <p>For normal use, yes. Saily's fair-use policy gives full speed up to 5GB in a rolling 24-hour window in most destinations (3GB in some), then throttles to 1Mbps until the local-midnight reset. Airhub doesn't offer unlimited plans, so this only applies to Saily.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which one gives me a local phone number</h3>
      <p>Airhub, in many destinations, bundled with the data plan, though it covers SMS only and not voice calls. Saily doesn't include a local number at all. For a number with calls guaranteed, see SimLocal's UK plans instead.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I use one eSIM across multiple countries on either provider</h3>
      <p>Yes for Saily: it's one universal eSIM that keeps working as you cross into a new supported country. Airhub sells a package per country, region, or global, so a multi-country trip usually means picking a regional or global package upfront rather than swapping per border.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

